## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to translate a compliance feature into a concrete, time-bound cost-benefit analysis suitable for a beginner. It tests how assumptions drive ROI and how to articulate go/no-go criteria.

## Key Concepts

- Cost estimation basics for small features
- ROI calculation and break-even timing
- Risk penalties avoided and adoption uplift
- Simple, testable calculations and trade-offs

## Code Example

```javascript
function breakEvenMonths(laborRatePerHr, weeks, engineers, monthlyOverhead, annualBenefit) {
  const upfront = laborRatePerHr * weeks * engineers * 40;
  const monthlyBenefit = annualBenefit / 12;
  const netMonthlyBenefit = monthlyBenefit - monthlyOverhead;
  if (netMonthlyBenefit <= 0) return Infinity;
  return Math.ceil(upfront / netMonthlyBenefit);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adjust for ARR vs one-time benefits?
- What metrics would you track after launch to validate forecasts?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to translate a feature into a practical 12‑month CBA with edge constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Edge inference costs (latency, energy, memory)
- Privacy and data minimization
- Cloud review costs vs offline results
- Rollout strategy and drift/maintenance costs
- Break-even horizon selection

## Code Example
```javascript
function estimateCBA(modelSizeMB, perInferenceJ, inferencesPerMonth, cloudCostPerEvent) {
  const energyKJ = perInferenceJ * inferencesPerMonth;
  const yearlyEnergyCost = (energyKJ / 1000) * 0.9;
  const cloudYear = cloudCostPerEvent * inferencesPerMonth * 12;
  const total = modelSizeMB + yearlyEnergyCost + cloudYear;
  return total;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you quantify drift/maintenance costs and retraining cadence?
- How would latency targets influence model size vs accuracy trade-offs?
- What metrics would you track to decide go/no-go?

